You just launched one of the most popular consumer tools of all time.

25
00:02:09,230 --> 00:02:14,852
And so the only thing that I want to know is what's the future that you're working toward with that tool?

26
00:02:15,118 --> 00:02:21,412
And how can this audience that's really optimistic and really wants to be part of making sure that that future go right?

27
00:02:21,742 --> 00:02:25,700
How can they feel like they're part of the decisions that are being made?

28
00:02:26,318 --> 00:02:27,120
Yep. All of that.

29
00:02:27,438 --> 00:02:27,920
That's what we do.

30
00:02:28,174 --> 00:02:30,560
One of the reasons I wanted to talk to you was

31
00:02:31,566 --> 00:02:34,560
I've always appreciated that you're really blunt.

32
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:44,240
Like I I honestly think that people need straight talk about this. I think that they feel confused.

33
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:49,040
And I want to get at what's really happening, what have you built?

34
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:51,760
How are people using it? How are they not?

35
00:02:52,014 --> 00:02:53,600
What are the real challenges?

36
00:02:54,030 --> 00:03:00,240
I think one other interesting thing is given how popular Muse has become, I want to use it as

37
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:03,760
a way to talk about other things in AI.

38
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:05,760
Yep. Awesome. Okay, let's get into it.

39
00:03:06,094 --> 00:03:09,280
Muse is the fastest growing consumer tool since ChatGPT.

40
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:11,120
Faster than ChatGPT, I think.

41
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:17,572
And it feels like this is the moment when we're moving, maybe, from chatbots to agents.

42
00:03:18,190 --> 00:03:21,360
But there have been a lot of other popular agents, mostly in code.

43
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:26,436
And I'm curious, why do you think this is happening for Muse right now? Why is Muse exploding like this?

44
00:03:26,990 --> 00:03:36,484
I think one of the mysteries of AI so far this year, for most of the year, has been that Agents were happening for engineers and software engineers and coders.

45
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:42,880
And then nobody else in the world was experiencing it, knew what it was, understood exactly what was happening.

46
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:52,400
And so for most of this year, you've kind of had this two dialogues almost where people who do code and could see how powerful AI was becoming and

47
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:56,480
how useful it was becoming were like, oh my gosh, this is incredible.

48
00:03:56,782 --> 00:04:03,972
And then everyone else was kind of like, what are you talking about? I still use a chat bot. Like it's like a little bit better than Google, but what are you guys all freaked out about?

49
00:04:05,710 --> 00:04:15,440
and one of the things that we were really excited to do is how do we take the magic that engineers and coders and developers are seeing and bring that to everyone?

50
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:25,040
One of the things that has happened over the course of the past year is like the AI models started out as pretty janky agents, and you kind of had to struggle through them to get them to work.

51
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:27,760
And then they slowly got better and better and better.

52
00:04:28,014 --> 00:04:33,040
And most of the industry has been really focused on making them just like better and better and better at coding.

53
00:04:33,230 --> 00:04:38,000
But we really felt this was like a big gap because most people in the world they don't know how to code.

54
00:04:38,080 --> 00:04:46,080
They probably never even want to code, but they still want to feel the experience of agents and having AI that's

55
00:04:46,080 --> 00:04:54,080
able to do more for you and actually start taking on more end-to-end workflows and take on more tasks for you. So that was really the goal behind Muse.

56
00:04:54,862 --> 00:05:03,920
And I think we spent a lot of time polishing all the little details to make sure that it would just work for people because we knew that like

57
00:05:04,526 --> 00:05:12,800
developers can kind of withstand a lot of jank and a lot of stuff breaking, and like they can kind of deal with all of that because you know, they're they're it's technology.

58
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:15,600
They're used to dealing with, you know, unripe technology.

59
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:20,260
But for everyone, you have to make something that's really smooth and really works for them.

60
00:05:20,654 --> 00:05:30,320
That also helps explain the big gap, I think, between what it feels like for most people to actually use AI when they were using a chatbot in particular,

61
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:35,600
and the conversation around AI that it's going to take everyone's jobs, it's really scary.

62
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:43,200
there's a macro conversation happening, and then there's an experience that people are having. And it just seems like those things are really, really far apart.

63
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:48,548
And to your point, Muse is giving more people the opportunity to have the insight that

64
00:05:49,262 --> 00:05:51,876
computer engineers had, you know, call it a year ago.

65
00:05:52,480 --> 00:05:52,880
Is that fair?

66
00:05:53,134 --> 00:05:57,600
I I think that's true. Yeah. Yeah. There are also moments in history that I think feel similar.

67
00:05:57,840 --> 00:06:01,440
Would you say it's like the mobile phone moment for agents?

68
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:09,440
There are, you know, billions of people brought online by mobile phones that never had PCs because it was easy and simple to use and they didn't, you know.

69
00:06:09,760 --> 00:06:17,680
I actually hadn't thought about that until you just mentioned it, but I think it's a great analogy because it's these models, they're improving really fast and they're improving really,

70
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:18,372
really quickly.

71
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:29,200
And it's really important that we, as those models get better, that the superpowers that they lend can be given to like literally everyone all around the world.

72
00:06:29,454 --> 00:06:31,300
The part that I like about the analogy is like

73
00:06:32,686 --> 00:06:40,900
so much of it is just about figuring out how to package it all together into a form factor that will resonate with people.

74
00:06:41,198 --> 00:06:45,280
And that's what we spend a lot of time trying to craft and get right and make perfect.

75
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:53,280
Which also gets to one of the interesting initial reactions that I've heard of Muse, which is is everybody's life more complicated than mine?

76
00:06:53,600 --> 00:07:01,360
Like I wake up, I go to work, I play with my kids, I have one restaurant reservation a month, I plan one vacation with my spouse.

77
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:04,644
Like, why are all these billionaires obsessed with restaurant reservations?

78
00:07:05,070 --> 00:07:06,400
Why do I need an assistant?

79
00:07:07,086 --> 00:07:08,480
How would you answer that?

80
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:15,748
Whenever I explain Muse to someone just who knows nothing about it, I just say like muse can help save you money and save you time.

81
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:20,240
And everybody in the world wants to save money and wants to save time.

82
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:24,640
Depending on what your lifestyle is, that can mean a different thing to you.

83
00:07:24,880 --> 00:07:32,640
I think almost everyone has like subscriptions that they shouldn't be paying for, or subscriptions that are really expensive, like cable bills or insurance.

84
00:07:32,880 --> 00:07:40,960
And for a lot of people, their magic moment with Muse is just saving money on things that they shouldn't be spending money on, or they shouldn't be spending so much money on,

85
00:07:41,102 --> 00:07:45,280
or finding gift cards that they hadn't spent, or, you know, the list goes on and on and on.

86
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:47,520
I think it's awesome that so many people are experiencing that.

87
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:56,640
I think that's like great because these are things that they're just, I think for most people, they're just so boring that you would never get to actually like handling them or

88
00:07:56,800 --> 00:08:00,240
dealing with them. But you know, your agent Muse can just do that for you.

89
00:08:00,462 --> 00:08:09,092
And then they're saving time, which I think for for lots of people, I think there are always experiences that are just way more frustrating than they should be. Like going to the DMV is one example.

90
00:08:09,280 --> 00:08:19,396
We actually, when we built Muse, we like specifically tested it to see if it could deal with the DMV website because that was like the pinnacle we thought of like

91
00:08:19,630 --> 00:08:23,972
the most frustrating experience for any human is dealing with a DMV.

92
00:08:24,206 --> 00:08:28,960
So we knew we needed to have Muse like be able to do well in that situation.

93
00:08:29,198 --> 00:08:32,640
We've seen people use it for like, yeah, navigating the healthcare system is a big one.

94
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:40,160
Medical claims is like a huge one that like most people just it's like so complicated and annoying that it's just like incredibly frustrating.

95
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:43,440
We've seen people like use it to help run their small business.

96
00:08:43,600 --> 00:08:53,120
We've seen a lot of people like use it to help run their social media, even. And I think the medical system is a really good example because it's like so hard to understand.

97
00:08:53,390 --> 00:08:57,840
And I think that like you know, one of the best ways to put it is like I think the cases where people,

98
00:08:58,190 --> 00:09:06,080
where it's like a huge lifesaver, are the cases where like otherwise you would be at your computer, you'd have to Google like 50 different things, you have to read,

99
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:09,828
go to like a hundred different websites and try to figure out what the hell is going on.

100
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:15,684
And it could, it's just like this incredibly frustrating experience, or you could have your muse do it.

101
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,420
I also think about how many things I suspect are made

102
00:09:21,646 --> 00:09:26,560
difficult and time consuming on purpose in order to frustrate me so much that I don't get to the end.

103
00:09:26,734 --> 00:09:30,880
How many claims are actually designed to be that hard so that I give up?

104
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:34,628
And I wonder what effect more people having more

105
00:09:35,438 --> 00:09:40,100
patience because they have support will have on that kind of systems engineering.

106
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:47,520
One of the little memes that I made of the little muse was uh muse and hood, which is like a muse Robinhood.

107
00:09:47,680 --> 00:09:56,000
But I think there's like a real truth to this, which is there are lots of things that exist in the world that are, to your point,

108
00:09:56,240 --> 00:09:57,440
meant to be hard.

109
00:09:57,678 --> 00:10:05,440
So that like uh canceling subscriptions is one of the best examples where there's some subscriptions where you have to like click on one thing and click on another thing,

110
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:10,800
and then click on like the little piece of text, and then not click the big red button, but click the other button.

111
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:17,444
And like that's just like the perfect example where there's lots of cases where things are designed to be hard for people.

112
00:10:17,920 --> 00:10:23,760
And I think like this is giving people the tools to be able to just do those things seamlessly and easily.

113
00:10:24,160 --> 00:10:25,540
I got access to the calling.

114
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:28,868
and I had it make a call this morning, which it did successfully.

115
00:10:29,230 --> 00:10:31,040
What does it sound like on the other end?

116
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:39,120
I think it's clear for the other end that it is like an AI. And I think if you read the, you know, we show we show users the transcript.

117
00:10:39,200 --> 00:10:44,640
Like if you see the transcript, like the beginning of the call I always think is really funny because it's like, hi, I'm so and so.

118
00:10:45,120 --> 00:10:55,652
I'm Cleo's assistant, and this call is being recorded. I think it's just like always this very funny beginning of a call. Hi, I'm Haley, calling on Cleo Abrams' behalf.

119
00:10:56,014 --> 00:10:57,520
I'm transcribing our call for notes.

120
00:10:58,030 --> 00:11:03,140
What are some examples of when it's messed up for you or when it's you feel like categories it struggles for still?

121
00:11:03,520 --> 00:11:08,228
One thing that we're always trying to improve is the memory

122
00:11:09,454 --> 00:11:19,556
of these agents, right? And this is still one of the more unsolved problems, I think, for a lot of these agents is having them, you know, the memory of the agents is like not like that of a human.

123
00:11:20,334 --> 00:11:23,200
sometimes it can feel like it's like that, but it is really different.

124
00:11:23,406 --> 00:11:28,400
So that's one area where we're like always trying to improve. We always want to want to figure out how to make the agents better.

125
00:11:28,560 --> 00:11:33,680
And then like we want them to be able to continue doing more and more complex tasks.

126
00:11:33,840 --> 00:11:39,760
I find this really funny, but there's um there's a lot of people who will just like ask their muse, I want you to make me $10,000.

127
00:11:40,240 --> 00:11:43,440
And obviously, not everyone can just ask their muse to make them $10,000.

128
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:51,280
But like I think, I think these kinds of like more amorphous, nebulous tasks, like we want Muse to be able to at least like build a plan and figure out.

129
00:11:51,360 --> 00:12:02,052
And, you know, you're going to have to do your part if you want to make $10,000. But I think we want to enable the agent to do more and more kind of complex and esoteric tasks to help the users.

130
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:08,960
There are some things where I feel emotionally, even if Muse could do them, I would prefer that they didn't.

131
00:12:09,040 --> 00:12:14,960
So for example, if I'm texting a friend and I find out that their Muse agent is responding to me, I would be offended by that.

132
00:12:15,120 --> 00:12:20,720
Are there categories of things that you think explicitly Muse should not do that are

133
00:12:21,520 --> 00:12:27,012
just like extremely deprioritized, not because they're technically that hard, but because socially not the point?

134
00:12:27,566 --> 00:12:29,220
A lot of what we care about is like

135
00:12:29,870 --> 00:12:38,720
having Muse kind of like clear the way such that you can spend more time on the things that you want to do and the things that you enjoy doing and the things that are like valuable to you.

136
00:12:38,960 --> 00:12:43,396
We generally speaking, want to figure out how to make the like

137
00:12:44,046 --> 00:12:46,800
someone's muse is like a different entity than them.

138
00:12:47,022 --> 00:12:49,360
That's why it's like a little cute guy.

139
00:12:50,560 --> 00:12:58,000
and and we want to over time continue making that more and more clear because to your point, like it is socially weird if you're like

140
00:12:58,080 --> 00:13:03,280
you think you're talking to your friend and it just happens to be the little muse, the little muse guy behind the behind the curtain.

141
00:13:03,680 --> 00:13:11,440
Speaking of what they look like, one of the things I've visited humanoid robots before, and one of the things they spend a lot of time thinking about is how to make it cute

142
00:13:11,534 --> 00:13:14,560
so that you like them and you want them in your home, or at least you're not afraid of them.

143
00:13:14,734 --> 00:13:20,160
What did Muse look like in previous iterations? And how did you decide on that particular guy?

144
00:13:20,430 --> 00:13:22,340
I think this is like one of the most shocking things.

145
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:27,972
What the Muse looks like now is what he looked like in the very first prototype.

146
00:13:28,206 --> 00:13:36,720
It's kind of one of these like rare things where the very first version was just perfect and then just like grew on all of us and we ended up going with it.

147
00:13:36,880 --> 00:13:45,828
We had lots of debates about it, which are, you know, in retrospect will be some of my funniest meetings of like, you know, a bunch of serious adults getting in a room, like

148
00:13:46,574 --> 00:13:50,400
arguing about this cute little you know, character.

149
00:13:51,598 --> 00:13:57,360
but yeah, I mean, I think we were like, is it not serious enough? Is it like too playful? Does it need to be more colorful?

150
00:13:57,520 --> 00:14:01,680
Like these were like all things that we asked the questions on and we looked at them, we're like, no, he's perfect.

151
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:09,280
What is the big vision here? I sometimes struggle when talking to AI leaders to push people to think about more than like five years out.

152
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:21,092
But what is what is the vision here? Is it that Muse becomes my layer on everything on the internet? Is it like map this out for me if it goes the way that you hope it does?

153
00:14:21,774 --> 00:14:29,636
What we really want Muse to be over time is kind of this like general manager, so to speak, that helps

154
00:14:30,160 --> 00:14:33,280
you accomplish all the things you want out of life.

155
00:14:33,520 --> 00:14:41,220
So, like, I think, I think one of the kind of tragedies of the modern world is that, like,

156
00:14:42,446 --> 00:14:46,480
most people have wants and dreams

157
00:14:47,470 --> 00:14:50,800
that can range from like really big ideas. like, "Hey.

158
00:14:50,880 --> 00:14:58,800
I want to start a company ," or "I, want to cure a disease ," or "I, want to save the planet ," all the way to, you know, small wants that are like, "Hey.

159
00:14:58,880 --> 00:15:03,600
I want to spend more time with my family and I want to eat healthier" and, you know, um.

160
00:15:03,950 --> 00:15:05,764
and and there's a whole range.

161
00:15:06,080 --> 00:15:08,740
but for most people, I think there's just so many

162
00:15:09,710 --> 00:15:11,440
barriers and burdens in the way.

163
00:15:11,600 --> 00:15:21,092
What I really hope it accomplishes is help every single one of the 8 billion people all around the world be able to accomplish their wants and dreams.

164
00:15:21,760 --> 00:15:22,080
And

165
00:15:23,470 --> 00:15:34,788
I think this is like, you know, if we are able to accomplish that, like I think the world will seem insane. That's what we really want Muse to be and what we think it can be if we keep working at it.

166
00:15:35,278 --> 00:15:43,940
You know, it's really nice to hear you talk about giving people time back because for a really long time, I have felt. and I think a lot of people have felt like

167
00:15:45,344 --> 00:15:54,544
so much of the financial incentive of most tech products that I use, especially the ones that are ad-backed, is to take my time,

168
00:15:54,704 --> 00:15:56,944
is to distract me from things that I want.

169
00:15:57,390 --> 00:15:58,464
Muse is free right now.

170
00:15:58,670 --> 00:16:08,964
It probably will have ads in the future. Is there a tension between giving people their time back and having an ads-based business? Or are there other incentives

171
00:16:09,710 --> 00:16:10,820
about like Muse

172
00:16:11,566 --> 00:16:18,212
steering me down a path that isn't quite what I said I wanted because it's ad-backed? How do you add ads to a product like this?

173
00:16:18,704 --> 00:16:23,024
First off, like I think right now it's free. There's subscription tiers.

174
00:16:23,264 --> 00:16:31,104
I think we think that there's plenty of um business models that are really aligned with the user around, like, for example.

175
00:16:31,184 --> 00:16:39,588
if they um, if they're buying things using Muse or Muse is saving them, money or Muse is making them money, like figuring out a way to

176
00:16:40,430 --> 00:16:43,268
take some small fee on on those kinds of transactions.

177
00:16:43,664 --> 00:16:49,024
Um so I wouldn't say we're spending that much time thinking about

178
00:16:49,984 --> 00:16:51,504
ads necessarily right now.

179
00:16:51,886 --> 00:16:58,224
That being said, like I don't think ad-based models are necessarily synonymous with like you know needing to take all of your time.

180
00:16:58,304 --> 00:17:07,664
I think Google ads are a really good example of this, where when I go to Google, my dream is to get in and get out. And I don't think Google's really trying to stop me from getting in and getting out.

181
00:17:08,064 --> 00:17:13,252
But they've built something obviously pretty incredible. And so yeah, I don't think it's a necessity by any means.

182
00:17:13,824 --> 00:17:17,508
I think, again, I think if we actually accomplish

183
00:17:18,158 --> 00:17:21,892
this thing that I'm saying, which is people are using Muse to

184
00:17:23,024 --> 00:17:29,412
really do the things that they want to do and really in a non-corny way,

185
00:17:30,478 --> 00:17:31,716
accomplish their dreams.

186
00:17:32,238 --> 00:17:32,544
I think

